# Create Filter
## Overview
A skill to create a filter component and manage the values of each filter field.

An overview of the process:
- Write seperate functional components for each filter field
- Wrap them all in a <Form></Form> for value management
```tsx
export const FeatureNameFilter = ({ props }: { props: PropsType }) => {
  return (
    <Form>
      // Place filter component renders here
    </Form>
  )
}
```
- Initialize the filterStore in the corresponding data source store
- Determine if the filtering is done by Front-End(FE) or Back-End(BE)
  - If FE, write a filter function in the same store
  - If BE, initialize the filterStore with the api call for the data source
- Check if there is also a paginationStore in the same store, reset current page to 1 on filter

## Component Implementation
Since a filter is usually part of a bigger page, and will always come with another data source component, place this filter component under the path ../{feature-name}/components/filter/index.tsx

Example of a filter functional component
Use an enumeration for Form.Item name, this enum will be constructed from
```ts
  - Pick<Partial<ReqParamType>, 'filterField1' | 'filterField2' | 'etc...'>
```
Since the filters are wrapped in a <Form></Form>, there is no need to use the value and onChange props to manage filter values
  - DO use the onChange if there are any side-effects involved when changing a filter field

### Input Example
Placeholder for Input is Enter
```tsx
const renderNameFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={EFilterField.name} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.name]}>
      <Input {...props} />
    </Form.Item>
  )
}
```
For input filters, remember to create a debounced api call function in store to call to prevent excessive api calls when user is typing
```tsx
import debounce from 'lodash/debounce';

debouncedApiCall = debounce(apiCall, 400);
```
and pass this debouncedApiCall into the filterStore initialization

### Select Example
Placeholder for Select is Select
The options for the <Select /> component should be a get() in the data source store
```tsx
@computed
get filterFieldSelectOption() {
  return this.dataSource?.map(data => ({
    label: `${data?.projectName} (${data?.projectCode})`,
    value: data?.projectCode
  })) || [];
}
```

```tsx
const renderIdcRegionFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={EFilterField.idcRegion} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.name]}>
      <Select {...props} />
    </Form.Item>
  )
}
```

### Radio Example
The options for the <Radio.Group /> component should be a get() in the data source store
Default selection will be the first option
```tsx
@computed
get filterFieldRadioOption() {
  return this.dataSource?.map(data => ({
    label: data?.projectName,
    value: data?.projectCode
  })) || [];
}
```

```tsx
const renderIdcRegionFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={EFilterField.idcRegion} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.name]}>
      <Radio.Group {...props}>
        {store?.filterNameRadioOption?.map((value, label) => <Radio.Button value={value}>{label}</Radio.Button>)}
      </Radio.Group>
    </Form.Item>
  )
}
```

### Filters with Inter-Filter logic Example
Some filters will cause a change in render/value in another filter
It is necessary to wrap the current filter in a <Form.Item noStyle shouldUpdate></Form.Item>

```tsx
const renderNameFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item noStyle shouldUpdate>
      {({ getFieldValue, setFieldValue, validateFields }) => {
        return (
          <Form.Item name={EFilterField.name} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.name]}>
            <Input onChange={(value) => {
              // Case where you need to reset another field on this field's change
              setFieldValue(EFilterField.otherField, undefined);

              // Case where you need to validate another field on this field's change
              validateFields({[EFilterField.otherField]});

              // Case where you perform an action if this value after change matches another field's value
              if (getFieldValue(EFilterField.name) === value) return;
              }
            }
            />
          </Form.Item>
        )
      }}
    </Form.Item>
  )
}
```

### Extendable Filters
If you discover that there are filter fields that have **total** logic overlap, consider writing a reuseable functional component render, and use props to manage to render differences

In this scenario, consider the following code block 
```tsx
const renderProjNameFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={EFilterField.projectName} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.projectName]}>
      <Input onChange={value => store.filterStore.setSingleFilter(EFilterField.projectName, value)} />
    </Form.Item>
  )
}

const renderTeamNameFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={EFilterField.teamName} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.teamName]}>
      <Input onChange={value => store.filterStore.setSingleFilter(EFilterField.teamName, value)} />
    </Form.Item>
  )
}

const renderTopicNameFilter() {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={EFilterField.topicName} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[EFilterField.topicName]}>
      <Input onChange={value => store.filterStore.setSingleFilter(EFilterField.topicName, value)} />
    </Form.Item>
  )
}
```
Since all 3 input filters have no further logics beyond filtering, we can instead use a common render function to reduce the amount of code debt in the repo
```tsx
const renderInputFilter(filterField: EFilterField) {
  return (
    <Form.Item name={filterField} label={FILTER_LABEL_MAP[filterField]}>
      <Input onChange={value => store.filterStore.setSingleFilter(filterField, value)} />
    </Form.Item>
  )
}

const renderFilter() {
  <Form>
    {renderInputFilter(EFilterField.projectName)}
    {renderInputFilter(EFilterField.teamName)}
    {renderInputFilter(EFilterField.topicName)}
  </Form>
}
```
